# REGIONS: slug,title,dates,intro,sections
REG=[
("africa","Africa","Africa is home to some of the oldest art in the world, from Blombos Cave engravings in South Africa to the Sahara rock art of Tassili n'Ajjer. It includes Egypt and Nubia in the north, as well as the many sculpture traditions of West, Central and Southern Africa.",
 [("Ancient Egypt and Nubia","Egypt's monuments dominate public imagination, but Nubian kingdoms such as Kush also built pyramids and temples."),
  ("West African sculpture","The terracotta Nok figures (~1000 BCE–300 CE), the bronzes of Ife and Benin, and later masks of many peoples show a wide range of styles."),
  ("Great Zimbabwe","Dry-stone walls built between about the 11th and 15th centuries."),
  ("Textiles and modern art","Kente cloth, bogolanfini and Ethiopian illuminated manuscripts sit alongside modern artists like El Anatsui and Sokari Douglas Camp."),
  ("Restitution","Many works were taken during colonial rule; museums and governments are negotiating returns.")]),
("asia","Asia","Asia spans several of the world's longest continuous art traditions.",
 [("China","Bronzes of the Shang dynasty, the Terracotta Army, ink landscape painting, calligraphy and porcelain. Landscape painting reached a peak in the Song dynasty (960–1279)."),
  ("Japan","From Jōmon pottery to Buddhist sculpture, Zen ink painting, ukiyo-e prints and contemporary art by Kusama and Murakami."),
  ("India and South Asia","Rock-cut temples at Ajanta and Ellora, Chola bronzes, Mughal miniature painting and the Taj Mahal."),
  ("Southeast Asia","Borobudur in Java and Angkor Wat in Cambodia are the greatest temple complexes."),
  ("West and Central Asia","Persian miniatures, Islamic architecture and the ancient Near East. Silk Road cities linked these traditions to China.")]),
("americas","The Americas","Indigenous peoples of the Americas developed monumental architecture, textiles and metalwork long before European contact, and their traditions continue today.",
 [("Mesoamerica","Olmec colossal heads (~1200–400 BCE), Maya stelae and murals, Teotihuacan and the Aztec Templo Mayor."),
  ("Andes","Chavín, Nazca (with its geoglyphs), Moche ceramics and Inca stonework such as Machu Picchu. Andean textiles encoded status and meaning."),
  ("North America","Pueblo pottery, Northwest Coast totem poles, Plains hide painting and Mississippian mound builders at Cahokia."),
  ("Colonial and modern","Colonial Baroque, 20th-century Mexican muralism, and today's Indigenous and Latinx artists.")]),
("oceania","Oceania","Oceania includes Australia, Melanesia, Micronesia and Polynesia, with art ranging from rock art tens of thousands of years old to monumental carving.",
 [("Australia","Aboriginal rock art is among the oldest continuous art traditions on Earth. The Papunya Tula movement (from 1971) brought Western Desert painting to world attention."),
  ("Polynesia","Moai on Rapa Nui, Māori carving (whakairo) and tattoo (tā moko), and Hawaiian featherwork."),
  ("Melanesia","Sepik River carvings, Asmat shields and elaborate masks of Papua New Guinea and Vanuatu."),
  ("Navigation and art","Stick charts from the Marshall Islands show swells and islands, an art of wayfinding.")]),
]
# THEMES: slug,title,intro,paragraphs,work slugs
THEMES=[
("religion","Religion and the sacred","Much of the world's art was made to honor gods or to guide worship.",
 ["Egyptian tomb painting prepared the dead for the afterlife. Greek temples housed images of gods. Gothic cathedrals aimed to bring heaven to earth with light.","Different faiths solve the problem of picturing the divine differently: icons in Orthodox Christianity, geometric pattern and calligraphy in Islam, mandalas in Buddhism.","Even after art left the church, it kept sacred themes, from Caravaggio's saints to Rothko's chapel."],
 ["hagia-sophia","chartres-cathedral","sistine-ceiling","ecstasy-of-st-teresa","dome-of-the-rock","borobudur","angkor-wat"]),
("power","Power and politics","Rulers have used art to proclaim authority, and artists have used it to question authority.",
 ["The stele of Hammurabi and the Augustus of Prima Porta both show rulers receiving divine or ideal authority.","Revolution brought propaganda (David's Marat) and protest (Goya's Third of May, Picasso's Guernica).","Contemporary artists such as Ai Weiwei and Banksy engage with politics directly."],
 ["hammurabi-stele","augustus-prima-porta","terracotta-army","death-of-marat","third-of-may-1808","guernica","sunflower-seeds"]),
("nature","Nature and landscape","Nature has been a subject, a source of materials and a mirror of feeling.",
 ["Ice Age painters drew the animals they depended on. Chinese landscape painters saw mountains as images of cosmic order.","Romantic painters made nature sublime, Impressionists studied its changing light, and modern architects such as Wright tried to merge with it.","Today, climate change is a major theme."],
 ["travelers-among-mountains","hunters-in-the-snow","rain-steam-and-speed","impression-sunrise","great-wave","fallingwater","starry-night"]),
("the-body","The human body","From fertility figures to athletes and self-portraits, artists keep returning to the body.",
 ["The Venus figurines may celebrate fertility. Greek sculptors idealized the athletic male nude. Michelangelo's David presents ideal strength.","Modern and contemporary artists question whose bodies are shown and how: Manet's Olympia stares back; Kahlo paints her own wounds.","Bourgeois and Kusama explore the body as memory and obsession."],
 ["discobolus","laocoon","david-michelangelo","olympia","two-fridas","maman"]),
("technology","Technology and change","New tools change what art looks like: oil paint, the printing press, photography, and now software.",
 ["Concrete let Romans build the Pantheon dome. Printing made Dürer's images widely available. The Eiffel Tower celebrated iron.","Photography pushed painters toward Impressionism and abstraction. Warhol used silkscreen to mimic mass production.","Digital artists like Beeple work with code, screens and networks."],
 ["pantheon","melencolia-i","eiffel-tower","rain-steam-and-speed","campbells-soup-cans","everydays-first-5000-days"]),
]
# TECHNIQUES: slug,title,text,works
TECH=[
("pigments","Pigments and paint","Pigments are colored powders mixed with a binder. Early artists used ochre, charcoal and manganese. Later came lapis lazuli blue, vermilion, lead white and, in the 19th century, synthetic colors. The choice of pigment and binder (egg, oil, acrylic) shapes a painting's look.",["standard-of-ur","hunters-in-the-snow"]),
("fresco","Fresco","Water-based pigment applied to wet plaster, so the color becomes part of the wall. Buon fresco demands speed: only the area that can be painted in a day is plastered.",["scrovegni-chapel","sistine-ceiling","school-of-athens"]),
("oil-painting","Oil painting","Pigment ground in drying oil such as linseed. Oil dries slowly, so it can be blended and built up in glazes. It spread from Northern Europe in the 15th century.",["arnolfini-portrait","mona-lisa","night-watch","girl-with-a-pearl-earring"]),
("bronze-and-casting","Bronze and casting","Metal poured into a mold. Lost-wax casting, used from ancient Greece to Ife and Benin, allows very fine detail.",["discobolus","ife-head","benin-plaques"]),
("carving-and-stone","Carving in stone","Sculptors remove material, revealing a form. Marble is prized for its translucence. Michelangelo spoke of freeing a figure from the block.",["david-michelangelo","great-sphinx","ecstasy-of-st-teresa"]),
("printmaking","Printmaking","Images printed from a prepared surface: woodcut, engraving, etching, lithography. Multiples let art circulate widely and cheaply.",["melencolia-i","great-wave"]),
("stained-glass","Stained glass","Colored glass pieces held in lead strips. In Gothic cathedrals it fills walls with changing light and tells Bible stories to those who could not read.",["chartres-cathedral"]),
("architecture-materials","Architecture and engineering","Materials and structural ideas shape buildings: the arch and dome in Rome, the flying buttress in Gothic Europe, iron in the 19th century, reinforced concrete in the 20th.",["pantheon","eiffel-tower","fallingwater","guggenheim-bilbao"]),
("digital-tools","Digital and mixed media","Software, video, mirrors, light and found objects. The ready-made and the installation expanded what materials count as art.",["fountain-duchamp","infinity-mirror-rooms","everydays-first-5000-days"]),
]
